[s0l0!-header][h1]Structures[/h1][/s0l0!-header]

A [b]structure[/b] is a [b]user-defined data type[/b] that groups related variables of different data types.

A structure [b]declaration[/b] includes the keyword [b]struct[/b], a [b]structure tag[/b] for referencing the structure, and curly braces { } with a list of variable declarations called [b]members[/b]. 
[b]For example:[/b][code format="c" ][b]struct [/b]course {
  int id;
  char title[40];
  float hours; 
}; [/code]
This struct statement defines a new data type named [b]course[/b] that has three members. 
Structure members can be of any data type, including basic types, strings, arrays, pointers, and even other structures, as you will learn in a later lesson.
[note]Do not forget to put a semicolon after structure declaration.
A structure is also called a [b]composite[/b] or [b]aggregate[/b] data type. Some languages refer to structures as [b]records[/b].[/note]

