[s0l0!-header][h1]Accessing Structure Members[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

You access the members of a struct variable by using the[b] . (dot operator)[/b] between the variable name and the member name.
For example, to [b]assign[/b] a value to the [b]age[/b] member of the [b]s1[/b] struct variable, use a statement like:
[b][/b][code format="c" ]s1.age = 19;[/code]
You can also assign one structure to another of the same type: [code format="c" codeId="1767"]struct student s1 = {19, 9, "Jason"};
struct student s2;
//....
s2 = s1; [/code]
The following code demonstrates using a structure: [code format="c" codeId="1768"]#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

struct course {
  int id;
  char title[40];
  float hours; 
};

int main() {
  struct course cs1 = {341279, "Intro to C++", 12.5};
  struct course cs2;

  /* initialize cs2 */
  cs2.id = 341281;
  strcpy(cs2.title, "Advanced C++");
  cs2.hours = 14.25;
   
  /* display course info */
  printf("%d\t%s\t%4.2f\n", cs1.id, cs1.title, cs1.hours);
  printf("%d\t%s\t%4.2f\n", cs2.id, cs2.title, cs2.hours);
  
  return 0;
}[/code]
String assignment requires [b]strcpy()[/b] from the string.h library. 
Also note the format specifiers [b]%4.2f [/b]include width and precision options.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

