The [b]typedef[/b] keyword creates a type definition that simplifies code and makes a program easier to read. 
[b]typedef[/b] is commonly used with structures because it eliminates the need to use the keyword [b]struct[/b] when declaring variables. 
[s0l0!-header][b]For example: [/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="1769"][b]typedef[/b] struct {
  int id;
  char title[40];
  float hours; 
} course;

course cs1;
course cs2; [/code]
Note that a structure tag is no longer used, instead a [b]typedef[/b] name appears before the struct declaration. 
Now the word struct is no longer required in variable declarations, making the code cleaner and easier to read.
